course: chemistry
lesson: 32
title: Recycling: giving materials a second life
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: After dinner you rinse a tin, flatten a cereal box and drop a bottle into the right bin. Each bin goes to a different place, because cans, paper, glass and plastic are each turned back into raw material in their own way.
explain: An aluminium drinks can is melted down and can be a new can back in the shop within about two months, using far less energy than digging up new rock. Paper is soaked and mashed into new paper, and plastic bottles can be spun into threads for fleece jackets.
explain: Recycling is good, but it still uses energy and trucks. Best of all is to reduce what we use, next to reuse things, and only then to recycle: that is why the saying goes "reduce, reuse, recycle" in that order.
explain: What is not recycled often goes to landfill, a huge hole where rubbish is buried and slowly rots, giving off a gas called methane.
fact: Each recycling bin collects a different material, because materials are recycled in different ways; dirty or mixed rubbish can spoil a whole load.
fact: An aluminium can can be recycled and be back on the shelf within about two months, and melting old cans needs far less energy than making aluminium from rock.
fact: Aluminium and glass can be recycled again and again without wearing out.
fact: Paper is soaked, mashed into pulp and pressed into new paper; its fibres get shorter each time, so paper can be recycled only a few times.
fact: Food scraps can be composted: microbes break them down into compost, which feeds plants.
fact: Old clothes can be passed on, sold second-hand or shredded into stuffing; clothes of mixed fibres are hard to recycle, and making new clothes uses lots of water.
fact: Batteries go in special collection boxes, never ordinary bins: they contain metals that harm nature if they leak and can be recovered, and batteries in bins can start fires in rubbish trucks.
fact: Plastic bottles can be melted and spun into fibres for fleece jackets; a jacket is often said to take about 25 bottles.
fact: Old phones contain small amounts of gold, silver and copper; a tonne of old phones holds far more gold than a tonne of rock from a gold mine.
fact: The order is reduce first, reuse second and recycle third, because recycling still uses energy; a reusable bottle can replace hundreds of throwaway ones.
fact: In landfill, rubbish rots without air and bacteria make methane, a gas that warms the planet; some landfills collect it to make electricity.
exercise: Why are there different bins for cans, paper and glass? | Each material is recycled in a different way | The bin men just like the different colours | Mixed bins are too heavy to lift | Each bin goes to exactly the same place anyway
exercise: Why can a greasy pizza box in the paper bin be a problem? | Dirty material can spoil a whole load | Paper bins can catch fire from the grease | Grease slowly turns the paper into plastic | It makes the bin far too heavy to empty
exercise: Why is recycling aluminium cans so worthwhile? | It needs far less energy than making new aluminium | Old cans are stronger and better than new ones | Cans turn into gold when melted | Recycled cans keep drinks colder for longer in summer
exercise: How long can it take for a recycled can to be back in the shop? | About two months | About two hundred years | About ten long years | About one hour or so
exercise: How is paper recycled? | It is soaked, mashed and pressed into new paper | It is melted down in a furnace like scrap metal | It is ironed flat and sold again | It is planted in the ground to grow new trees
exercise: Why can paper be recycled only a few times? | Its fibres get shorter each time | The ink cannot ever be taken out again | Paper turns to plastic in the water | Paper gets a little heavier each time
exercise: What turns food scraps into compost? | Microbes breaking them down | Sunlight drying them out slowly | Worms painting them dark brown | Salt added by the gardener each week
exercise: What is a good use for clothes you've grown out of? | Pass them on or take them for reuse | Burn them on a bonfire in the garden | Bury them in the back yard | Leave them in the rain
exercise: Why must old batteries go in a special box, not the bin? | They can leak harmful metals and start fires | They are far too heavy for the bin lorry to lift | They charge themselves in the bin | They make the bin smell bad
exercise: What can old plastic bottles be turned into? | Fibres for fleece jackets | Glass for new windows | Paper for exercise books | Metal for new bikes
exercise: Why are old phones worth recycling? | They contain gold, silver and copper | They can be turned into new food | They are made only of plastic | They charge other phones for free when in the bin
exercise: In "reduce, reuse, recycle", which comes first and why? | Reduce, because using less saves the most | Recycle, because it uses no energy at all | Reuse, because it is easiest | They are all exactly as good
exercise: What does a reusable water bottle do? | It replaces hundreds of throwaway bottles | It turns tap water into juice | It makes plastic bottles cheaper | It recycles itself every week
exercise: What happens to rubbish in a landfill? | It is buried and slowly rots, giving off methane | It is melted down and made into brand-new things | It disappears in a few days | It turns into soil in a week
exercise: What can some landfills do with their methane gas? | Collect it to make electricity | Bottle it for fizzy drinks | Let children breathe it | Use it to fill balloons
exercise: Which of these can be recycled again and again without wearing out? | Glass and aluminium | Paper and thin cardboard | Food scraps and old clothes | Batteries and nappies
test: Leo: "recycling's the best, so it doesn't matter how much stuff we buy". What's true? | Using less is better than recycling | He's right, recycling uses no energy | Recycling is worse than landfill | Buying more helps the recyclers
test: "just chuck the dead batteries in the normal bin, who cares". What could happen? | They could start a fire in the bin lorry | They would charge up again | Nothing, they are completely empty and quite safe | They would turn into compost
test: If everyone threw greasy food tubs into the paper bin, what would happen? | Loads of paper could be spoiled | Paper would recycle faster | The grease would clean the paper | The tubs would turn to paper
test: Grandad says a can thrown away is just a can. What could it become if recycled? | A new can in about two months | A new tree in a forest | A glass bottle in a year | Nothing, cans cannot be reused
test: A cartoon says landfill rubbish vanishes into the ground in a day. What really happens? | It rots slowly and makes methane | It turns into flowers by morning | It melts into oil at once | It is eaten by moles in a day
test: Your fleece jacket label says "made from recycled bottles". How is that possible? | Bottles are melted and spun into threads | Bottles are cut up and sewn together | Bottles are soaked and become wool | It is a trick, bottles cannot be reused
test: "old phone in the drawer's just junk mate". What's true? | It holds metals worth recovering, even gold | It is only plastic inside, nothing useful | It will rot away in a few years | It is worth more if you keep it in the drawer
test: If paper could be recycled forever, why do we still need new wood pulp? | Fibres get too short after a few times | Old paper turns to ink | New trees make paper whiter | Wood pulp is the only real paper
test: "chuck the apple core in the compost, yeah?" What happens to it there? | Microbes turn it into food for plants | It stays an apple core forever | It turns into a tree straight away, roots and all | It makes the compost poisonous
test: Mia's mum gives her old school uniform to a younger girl. Which step is this? | Reuse | Recycle | Landfill | Compost
test: Which bin should a clean empty glass jam jar go in? | The glass recycling bin | The food scraps bin | The battery box | The garden waste bin
exam: Identify the order of preference expressed in the principle "reduce, reuse, recycle". | Reduction first, reuse second and recycling third | Recycling first, reuse second and reduction third | All three are of strictly equal merit | Reuse first, recycling second and reduction third
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the recycling of aluminium? | It requires far less energy than production from ore | It requires more energy than production from ore | It can be performed only once | It converts the metal into glass
exam: It is given that refuse is deposited in a landfill site. Select the response that is consistent with the established facts. | It decomposes slowly and emits methane | It disappears entirely within days | It is spontaneously converted into compost | It emits no gas whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which spent batteries are excluded from general refuse. | They may leak harmful metals and ignite fires | They regain their charge when buried | They are excessively heavy for collection | They emit pleasant odours
